// Sandbox mission: break through to the enemy base and destroy it.
//
// A 2048x1536 map laid out the way the campaign maps do it (l05): the factories
// stand at the dead end of a straight spur that meets the line through a Y,
// and the repair workshops stand on a track that trains drive through.
//
//   - main line: from past the west edge of the map (level entry, where the
//     reinforcement arrives) along the bottom, up the east side and west along
//     the top to past the west edge again (level exit, one way into the map)
//   - the player's base (south-west): the reinforcement arrives past the west
//     end of the main line (the point locomotive, VID 5 army 3), the factory
//     stands at the top of a spur north of the line, the repair workshop on
//     the west leg of a loop next to it; every leg meets the line in a Y, so
//     trains reach the loop and leave it both ways, and the workshop leg is
//     one way (north through the workshop), as in l05
//   - a passing loop on the east line
//   - the enemy base (north): the factory at the bottom of a spur hanging from
//     the top line, the enemy repair workshop on a loop hanging from it (a Y at
//     each leg, the workshop leg one way south), and a fort with two guns - a
//     gate on the east line
//   - every one-way section (push line) has semaphores (VID 152) at its ends,
//     red for the trains it forbids, green for those it lets in (MAP_RULES.md)
//
// Push lines and patrol points are screen coordinates: the world y minus the
// height of the rail points (z 66 of the rails + 10 of their altitude = 76).
// ACT_MOVE of a script takes the world position (Engine_MoveToPoint, 3D).
//
// Goal: destroy every enemy unit that NoArmyUnits(1) counts (ARMY1_NVID_ARRAY in
// constants.lgc): the factory, the fort's two rocket launchers and the trains. The
// fort itself is a static object without hit points (U_OBJECT, maxHp 0) - the gate
// stays - and the workshops are not counted.
// Lose condition: the player has no units left and no factory to rebuild from.

// The two blocks between "// <editor>" and "// </editor>" and between "// <editor main>" and
// "// </editor main>" belong to the map editor (DEVELOPERS.md "Editor mode", as in
// maps\template\template.lgc): File > Mission settings... and the Push line tool change them and
// the editor writes them anew when the map is saved. The rest is the mission's own code.
//
// What the settings say: 300 money at the start, day, no rain and no lightning (daynight.lgc:
// on by default, it flashes the whole screen purple every few seconds), the gammas of mission
// 2. This map is not a numbered campaign level, so the objective/win/loss texts, the briefing
// of the Esc menu (its pages and header) and the high scores are read from and written to this
// folder instead of maps\lNN\ - otherwise LEVEL 0 would show mission 1's briefing and put a win
// into mission 1's high score table. CUSTOM_MAP marks a custom map, not a campaign mission
// (menuend.lgc, menutact.lgc, initdefault.lgc): a win does not unlock missions or add to the
// campaign scores, rank and hall of fame, "restart" in the Esc menu loads this map, and the
// main menu comes back on the custom maps page.
//
// Reinforcements: two waves of a locomotive and a light gun wagon. The enemy: two trains at
// the start, each patrolling a line (screen points: ATA_PATROL0 the east line, ATA_PATROL1 the
// east part of the bottom line) and rebuilt by its factory when lost (start_trains_renew), and
// two light gun trains its factory builds at 240 s and 420 s that attack (ATA_FIGHT), at most
// two attackers at once, 45 s between rebuilt trains.

// the cars of the enemy's train types (TrainsTactDeclares.lgc takes these instead of its own;
// they must come before it)
int this_train0[]={20, 5, 20, 0};   // light gun train: gun wagon, loco, gun wagon
int this_train1[]={20, 10, 20, 85, 0};
int this_train10[]={25, 10, 25, 0}; // light mortar train
int this_train11[]={25, 10, 25, 85, 0};

